// Formative practice quiz for branches-and-switching. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/git-foundations/branches-and-switching

::branches-and-switching o1 - what a branch is::A classmate says that creating a branch makes a full copy of the project files in a new folder. What is a branch in Git?{
=A movable name that points at one commit#Correct. A branch is just a name for a commit. It moves forward when you commit while you are on it, so creating one is cheap.
~A separate copy of every project file#Not quite. Creating a branch copies no files; it adds a name that points at the commit you are on.
~A separate repository that has to be cloned#Not quite. Branches live inside the same repository. A branch is only a name pointing at a commit.
~A fixed label that always stays on the commit where it was created#Not quite. That describes a fixed marker, not a branch. A branch moves forward to each new commit made while HEAD is on it.
}

::branches-and-switching o2 - create and move onto a branch::You are on main in a local-only repository. No branch named search exists yet. You want to create search and be on it right away, in one command. What do you run?{
=git switch -c search#Correct. -c creates the branch at the commit you are on and switches to it in one step.
~git branch search#Not quite. git branch search creates the branch but leaves HEAD on main, so your next commit would still move main.
~git switch search#Not quite. git switch moves HEAD to an existing branch. search does not exist yet, so Git reports an error instead of creating it.
~git switch main, then git branch search#Not quite. You are still on main afterwards. git branch creates the name without moving HEAD.
}

::branches-and-switching o3 - which name moves after git branch::You are on main at C4. You run git branch report and then, without switching, git commit -m "Add totals", which makes C5. Where do main and report point?{
=main points at C5; report stays at C4#Correct. git branch report created the name but did not move HEAD, so you were still on main. A commit moves only the branch HEAD is on.
~report points at C5; main stays at C4#Not quite. Creating a branch does not switch to it. HEAD was still on main, so main moved.
~Both point at C5#Not quite. Only the branch HEAD is on moves forward. Other branch names stay where they were.
~Neither moves; C5 is left without a branch#Not quite. The branch HEAD is on, main, always moves to the new commit.
}

::branches-and-switching o2 - switching swaps working-copy files::On branch feature you committed a new file login.html as C6. main is at C5, which does not have login.html. You run git switch main. What happens to login.html in your project folder?{
=It disappears from the working copy, and it is still safe in C6 on feature#Correct. Switching changes your working-copy files to match the branch you switch to. git switch feature brings the file back.
~It stays in the folder, because files are not tied to branches#Not quite. Committed files belong to the commits a branch points at, and switching makes the working copy match the new branch.
~It is deleted for good, because main never had it#Not quite. The file is still recorded in C6, which feature points at. Nothing is lost.
~Git refuses to switch until you delete login.html yourself#Not quite. The file is committed on feature, so Git can safely remove it from the working copy and restore it later.
}
